@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:architecture] hakuba-concerns-have-one-home
Each concern MUST live in its one home, and nowhere else:
- game code that runs when the game plays: `crates/<feature>/`, one Bevy plugin per crate;
- wiring the plugins into the app: `src/main.rs`, which holds no game logic;
- what an asset is (its source files and its own numbers, shapes and choices): that asset's `src/` under `assets/`;
- how every asset of a kind is made: `tools/export/`;
- what the game loads: the exports beside each asset's `src/`.
Code that would sit in a second home MUST move to its home instead of being copied.

@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:architecture] hakuba-is-self-contained
Hakuba's code, assets, docs and comments MUST stay in `games/hakuba` and MUST NOT import, read, name or
describe anything outside it, including other games and the `atelier` command. A clone of the repository
MUST build, export and run Hakuba with plain `cargo` and `trunk`.

@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:architecture] hakuba-everything-used-and-described
Every committed file MUST be used by the game or by its exports. Every folder and file MUST say what it is
for, in a README beside it or a header comment, describing only what is there now, in short, plain words.

@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:performance] sixty-on-the-reference-setting
The game MUST hold 60 frames a second in the browser on the reference setting `PERFORMANCE.md` names. A change that
can cost GPU time (a pass, a shader or material, many more meshes or vertices, a default setting) MUST be measured
there as that file says, with nothing else on the GPU, and its pull request MUST give the frame rate and the GPU time
of each pass that moved, before and after. A fix for a cost MUST add a check of its kind where one can be written
(as the errors for cut-out materials and crossfading ranges), so the next asset cannot bring it back.
